1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,320
Hi, if you're like me, you spent hours scrolling on TikTok or Pinterest looking at Minecraft building ideas without the intent to ever actually build them.

2
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:11,720
Now, I don't know about you, but sometimes inspiration just randomly hits.

3
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:15,580
And I have to play Minecraft. Like, that's all that's on my mind.

4
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:22,140
And lately I've been seeing a whole lot of cottagecore Minecraft content circulating around the internet, getting a lot of views.

5
00:00:22,460 --> 00:00:28,220
And so I downloaded some shaders and launched Minecraft at like 6fps and built this for nostalgia.

6
00:00:28,220 --> 00:00:36,060
And if you're like literally any person who's played Minecraft over the years, these houses were the bomb. They were like move-in ready.

7
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:42,000
Of course, there was only room for like three things to fit in there, and then there was this problem.

8
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:50,280
It's Minecraft cottagecore aesthetic because the mushroom houses with this aesthetic are like next level. They are chef's kiss. They are immaculate.

9
00:00:50,280 --> 00:01:00,520
And suddenly your wikiHow tutorial on how to make simple mushroom houses featuring acacia plank flooring are more anesthetic than the 2013 mustache trend.

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:08,100
And if this strangely specific scenario applies to you, then boy will you like this tutorial.

11
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:12,940
So now I present three ways to make mushroom houses in Minecraft.

12
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:15,100
Yay.

13
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:18,900
So the first house is pretty easy.

14
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:25,900
You're gonna want to start out with a statistically impossible mushroom in the middle of a field and use bone meal to make it big.

15
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:30,900
Now stare at that mushroom until you decide to mutilate it to look aesthetic, like this.

16
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:40,900
I wanted the mushroom to look a bit different than the other simple mushroom houses you typically see, so I went for more of a bell-shaped curve for the top of the mushroom.

17
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:44,900
You could also use one of those mini water bottles for reference.

18
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:51,120
I modeled mine based on an upside down flower pot, and I think it turned out pretty good.

19
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:56,900
Once you have achieved maximum upside down flower pot conformity, then you can start modifying

20
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:59,480
the stem to make it look stalkier.

21
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:04,460
I used the mushroom stem block and smooth court stairs, and if you're in survival you'll

22
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:08,420
probably want to mine a few of the big mushrooms with a silk touch axe to get the materials

23
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:09,420
for this.

24
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:13,180
I used birch plank flooring to match the rest of the interior.

25
00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:25,567
Now I really like this house but there not much room Sorry for my spore sense of humour Here the big grand reveal of this house Very simple I give it a 8 out of 10

26
00:02:25,567 --> 00:02:43,567
Next house. This was a little bit more difficult than the last one, and so you're gonna start out by making a frame that looks like the outline of a handmade cube of playdoh, the kind that the edges never seem sharp enough, and then start sculpting it to look like the body of this absurdly priced plastic Minnie Mouse teapot I found on Amazon.

27
00:02:43,567 --> 00:03:00,067
I use smooth quartz blocks, slabs, and stairs to accomplish this, and if you want to use mushroom stem blocks, I'm sure you could, but I just like quartz because it makes the mushroom stem seem smooth and continuous, sort of like a differentiable equation. Also because it's not a pain to get all those stems.

28
00:03:00,067 --> 00:03:10,067
After this, start making the stem taller to look almost like a BB-8, and then give the wannabe BB-8 some helicopter blades. These will be the radius of your circular mushroom cap.

29
00:03:10,067 --> 00:03:13,907
Then connect those little helicopter blades to form a circle.

30
00:03:13,907 --> 00:03:20,027
Also, I never really found these graphs useful, I always find them on like TikTok, but then

31
00:03:20,027 --> 00:03:23,507
again I always just wing it and it doesn't look very circular so maybe they're helpful

32
00:03:23,507 --> 00:03:26,187
to you, but mine turned out pretty great in the end.

33
00:03:26,187 --> 00:03:31,947
Anyway, start outlining the circle in red mushroom blocks, and then be indecisive, and then decide

34
00:03:31,947 --> 00:03:37,587
on making the easy way out, and make a circular spherical looking mushroom cap.

35
00:03:37,587 --> 00:03:42,587
Mine is about 6 blocks tall, and unlike me, you probably want to make sure there's no weird blocks left exposed.

36
00:03:42,587 --> 00:03:48,587
Then, I made the door and the window and outlined them in the most superior wood type, spruce wood.

37
00:03:48,587 --> 00:03:52,587
I used planks, fences, and stairs as well as oak leaves for the window boxes.

38
00:03:52,587 --> 00:03:57,587
To add some extra whimsy, you can make a dilapidated cobblestone chimney and drape that with oak leaves.

39
00:03:57,587 --> 00:04:04,587
To add some stuff to the interior, I connected the chimney to a fireplace furnace type thing

40
00:04:04,587 --> 00:04:20,507
I also used flower pots, turtle eggs, and sea pickles for the inside, and then I discovered that in 1.16.5 you can place turtle eggs on flower pots, which is kind of weird.

41
00:04:21,227 --> 00:04:29,767
Anywho, I used vines as a ladder to the attic area, which will be a cute little bedroom. I outlined the roof with spruce wood and then placed some bookshelves behind them.

42
00:04:29,767 --> 00:04:44,133
This would also be a cool enchanting room but the house demands a bedroom So I added in a skylight which looks good with the shaders and I sure it would look good without them So this is the finished build It only took me about 45 minutes to do in creative so not too bad

43
00:04:48,133 --> 00:04:54,133
Alright, on to the last one. This one is kinda hard, but not like prior gaming at the end of his TikToks kinda hard.

44
00:04:54,133 --> 00:04:59,133
You're gonna wanna start out with a circular base like such and then add a small circle attached to it.

45
00:04:59,133 --> 00:05:03,533
Once you have your two little circle buddies, start making them taller and rounder.

46
00:05:03,533 --> 00:05:07,373
I focused on the bigger circle first because it's going to be the centerpiece for the build.

47
00:05:07,373 --> 00:05:15,533
Also, if this video gets 100 likes, I'll make a video of my little sister following this tutorial to see how she does because she's bad at building and I'm bad at explaining things.

48
00:05:15,533 --> 00:05:25,613
Okay, back to the tutorial. If you don't know what shape to make the base of the big mushroom, I referred to the gourd from the 2007 Disney classic, Secret of the Magic Gourd, and mine turned out pretty alright.

49
00:05:25,613 --> 00:05:37,133
Also, I need to know, was anyone else low-key traumatized by that movie? Because when I first saw that movie, I guess I just thought gourds were strange pumpkins, so for the longest time I was fundamentally confused on what gourds were.

50
00:05:38,173 --> 00:05:46,173
So anyway, you're gonna start making another one of those helicopter circle things, and here's a better explanation using calculus if you want to know how to make circles in Minecraft.

51
00:05:46,893 --> 00:05:49,133
Presenting, building circles, yay!

52
00:05:49,133 --> 00:05:57,133
So you're gonna want to start out with this sort of graph paper, just trace something circular onto it, and then fill in the blocks that the circle crosses.

53
00:05:57,133 --> 00:06:04,133
Make sure it all has the same radius. And then this is where the calculus comes in. If you look at tangent lines, you know what tangent lines are, I hope.

54
00:06:04,133 --> 00:06:17,133
You probably don't. But the slope of the tangent line is sort of the slope of, if you were to draw the slope through the grid squares of the circle, this made a lot more sense inside my head.

55
00:06:17,133 --> 00:06:22,773
But you can trace pretty much any circle on the graph paper and repeat this process.

56
00:06:22,773 --> 00:06:26,273
Just make sure the radius is the same and that each quadrant is the same.

57
00:06:26,273 --> 00:06:28,693
And there you go, you have a circle in Minecraft.

58
00:06:28,693 --> 00:06:33,333
Okay, now moving on to our other little mushroom buddy.

59
00:06:33,333 --> 00:06:37,273
You're gonna want to make him a wonky little mushroom that sort of stems off from the big one.

60
00:06:37,273 --> 00:06:41,153
It's really hard to explain how I did this, but I would recommend using organic shapes

61
00:06:41,153 --> 00:06:43,893
and have the top of the mushroom thinner than the base.

62
00:06:43,893 --> 00:06:45,173
Then add a tiny little cap.

63
00:06:45,173 --> 00:07:12,700
For the top of the big mushroom I basically did the same thing as I did for the medium tutorial and despite it being vastly bigger and some hanging lanterns You could also use shroom lights I also added a dilapidated chimney to mash the vibe

64
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:19,820
And then for the interior, I started again by connecting the fireplace to the chimney and built a loft area with a ladder leading up to it.

65
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:24,220
And then I got distracted by a wandering trader and quickly resumed my work.

66
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:27,480
And I used an absurd amount of birch leaves to decorate.

67
00:07:28,420 --> 00:07:31,540
If you can't tell from my Pinterest page, birch leaves are my favourite.

68
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:36,320
Also, if you're wondering how they move in the wind, that's the shaders, and I use BSL shaders.

69
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:38,780
As far as I know, they only work for Java Edition.

70
00:07:38,780 --> 00:07:46,180
I made a little kitchen space in front of one of the windows, complete with some cake and some turtle eggs, and then added some seating by the fire.

71
00:07:46,740 --> 00:07:51,540
In front of the other window, I made what HGTV calls a breakfast nook, complete with some plants.

72
00:07:51,1000 --> 00:07:56,900
While the bedroom space is smaller than the previous build, I think it turned out nicely and I wouldn't mind living there.

73
00:07:57,420 --> 00:08:06,960
After decorating the bedroom, I remembered there was a bunch of empty space in the loft, which I promptly decorated with barrels because that can be practical when they're not converting all the villagers to fishers,

74
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:32,860
And I did notice I accidentally made a sort of shrine to Pinocchio, which is cool I guess. On the outside I was going for a house-ish entrance, but I ended up making it more like a hobbit hole with spruce trapdoors and slabs and stairs. I also added some dark oak for better contrast, and ended up expanding the windows downward because it just looked better. I added some shutters to the bedroom window and shroom lights to better illuminate it at night.

75
00:08:32,860 --> 00:08:45,480
There was something that was still missing though, and I couldn't decide what to do so I tried this thing I saw on TikTok where you make mushrooms out of end rods and mushroom blocks which I was a bit skeptical of, but I really liked how they turned out.

76
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:57,740
So yeah, those are three mushroom houses you could easily build in Minecraft. Comment below which one you'd live in, I know I definitely would pick the second one because of the skylight but that's just me.

77
00:08:57,740 --> 00:09:07,180
And if you enjoy this content and would like to see more dollar store green tutorials, please feel free to hit subscribe. See y'all later, bye!
